  "title": "landing: S2e worker_control.cancel: safe cancellation of a running worker with worktree and commits preserved, structur",
  "spec": "Land the reviewed change of T-1115 (branch task/T-1115, commits 8ed74a9; review verdict approve) onto the current goal/T-0991 head. In THIS worktree (fresh from goal/T-0991) cherry-pick the commits in order; resolve the conflicts (expected in tests/test_cli.py) by keeping BOTH sides' additions: the goal branch carries new subcommands, imports and tests from sibling tasks, the cherry-picked commits add this task's own; nothing is removed. Verify with the git log that the change sits on top of the goal head, then run .claude/hooks/tests-green.sh . until it exits 0. Do not change behaviour beyond conflict resolution; do not ask questions; report the resolved files and failures-only output.\nOriginal task spec for reference (already implemented in the cherry-picked commits):\nCreate orchestrator/worker_control.py. cancel(task_id, reason, *, source=\"planner\", grace_s=20) -> dict: registry status cancelling (event cancel_requested with reason and source); signal the worker process: for claude workers the registry pid (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after grace_s if still alive); for codex workers executor.py must record the codex exec subprocess pid in the registry at launch (edit executor.py: the Popen or run call that starts codex exec; pass the pid to worker_registry.upsert) so cancel can signal it the same way; never touch the worktree files; collect partial = partial_result(task) -> {files_changed: git status --porcelain paths in the worktree, diff_stat: git diff --stat against the task base (merge-base with the goal branch), commits: log of commits since the base (sha, subject), tests_run: the last gate output lines in the worktree if scripts/failures_only.sh output or a tests-green log exists, else [], errors: last 20 lines of the worker's stderr capture if the registry exit event kept a path, files_inspected: paths from registry tool events when present, unresolved: []} \u2014 observable evidence only, never prompt or transcript text; bus: bus.update(task_id, status=\"held\", hold_reason=\"cancelled\", pid=None, result={\"partial\": partial, \"cancel_reason\": reason, \"cancelled_by\": source, \"confidence\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [\"worker_partial\"]}) (check bus.STATUSES and post_result size cap; truncate lists to fit); pool release: pool.Pool().release(task_id, {}) as executor.py does; registry finish cancelled; return the partial. daemon.auto_fix_round must skip held tasks whose hold_reason is cancelled (they are the Planner's to re-spec) and reconcile_dead must not requeue a task whose registry status is cancelling or cancelled. CLI: `orchestrator workers cancel <task> --reason <text>` prints the partial summary. Docs: docs/hermes-hardening/11-worker-control.md (cancel sequence, what is preserved, what is never captured).\nSpec review T-1090 folded in: (1) orchestrator/worker_registry.py is in scope: extend its KINDS with cancel_requested, cancelled, steer, steered and its FIELDS with source and cancel_reason so cancel() records through the whitelist instead of raising. (2) cli.py: `workers` becomes a subcommand group whose default action (no subcommand) keeps today's `workers [--task T] [--all] [--json]` behaviour; `workers cancel <task> --reason <text>` is the nested subcommand; existing tests for `workers` must keep passing. (3) daemon.reconcile_dead checks worker_registry.get(task)['status'] explicitly and returns 'cancelled' without touching the bus when it is cancelling or cancelled; the tick snapshot race is covered by that check, not by bus status. (4) cancel(task_id, reason, *, source='planner', grace_s=20, sleep=time.sleep, alive=None, signal_fn=os.kill): injectable seams; the escalation test uses a fake sleep and a fake alive that flips after the grace to keep the test instant.\nSpec review T-1113 folded in: executor.py already records the codex subprocess pid at launch through worker_registry.event(..., 'spawned', pid=...) (executor.py near line 164) and spawn.py does the same for claude workers, so cancel() reads the pid from worker_registry.get(task_id) and executor.py is dropped from the scope (no executor edit).\nRepository rules: unittest (not pytest), tests/_harness.py single ORCH_ROOT fixture; every acceptance test id tests/test_<module>.py::test_name is a method def test_name(self) in a unittest.TestCase. No new third-party dependencies. Never write secret values to any file or log; test fixtures use synthetic names and values that are not credential-shaped. Do not quote template placeholder tokens (double braces) anywhere in code comments or docs. Shadow-first: a new adaptive path never changes what a worker sees until its mode is active; shadow only writes decision_log or runs rows. Run .claude/hooks/tests-green.sh . until green; the base suite must stay green. Do not ask questions: decide, and note decisions in the result summary. The interface contracts of the stage 1 modules are in .orchestrator/plan.md (section GOAL T-0991) and in docs/hermes-hardening/0*.md on this branch; read the merged module before building on it.",
